he is the commissioner of the pac-12  he's larry scott back on the program  commissioner good morning thanks for  joining us  what has changed since the last time we  spoke  well we announced yesterday a  groundbreaking  partnership with quiddell  california-based  testing company i was the first to be  approved for  what people refer to as these  point-of-care  rapid result tests where you basically  get a result in  15 minutes and we think this is a game  changer for  us because our medical advisors said  this is the only way they can be  confident that if we're asking student  athletes  to go practice in a high context sport  like football or basketball for example  the only way we can be confident we're  not incurred encouraging the spread  of the virus and that's kind of a  standard of care our medical advisors  and our presidents have felt that we  should apply  you know we're asking student athletes  to participate in activity it's not  recommended  by the cdc it's not a socially distanced  activity  so it's been a sense we've had and we've  been you know on one end of the spectrum  here  that there's a moral and ethical  responsibility for students to not put  them in  more harm's way than they'd be in  otherwise and by by being able to test  a student athlete every day before they  go into practice or  game we can have a high degree of  confidence  that no one in practice or a game has  the coronavirus and could spread it so  this is an important step in the path to  us reconsidering resumption of play  how soon is this test going to be  available so we um  you know signed and announced the  agreement yesterday we're going to have  all the materials sent to our schools uh  by the end of this month  and so by the beginning of october we're  going to be able to start  start the testing and we're also doing a  research project for the company  on uh you know how young mostly  asymptomatic  healthy student athletes what's the  impact of being able to test daily  because  you know our universities do a lot of  research you know we're working on  some of these vexing challenges with  covet and in athletics we're gonna have  a big test case  and uh you know there's a thesis that  this kind of daily testing with rapid  results  could also be the answer to return to  school for k-12 kids  return to work and you know society and  the economy getting back to normal so  there's another component of that too um  so you know but to answer your question  october we'll be in kind of full testing  mode  if the test goes well is the pac-12 in  business playing football this fall  um i believe we will be whether it's in  the fall or january first remains to be  seen  you know this is isn't unfortunately our  only challenge we've got six schools  currently then  the four california schools ucla usc  stanford and cal and two of our oregon  schools oregon and oregon state  that don't currently have government  approvals to have contact practice i  know  the approvals have been given for pro  sports but not for college  sports so we'll need some help from the  counties public health officials to  bless this and say it's okay  now i think what we announced yesterday  is gonna be an important piece of that i  think the public health officials are  keenly interested in what our plans and  protocols are  so i'm hopeful that we will get what we  need but as it is now you're not going  to play fall football  no as it is now uh we had made a  decision  um several weeks ago and this doesn't  change that  it could it does it doesn't yet uh again  we don't have government approvals yet  if we get the government approvals and  uh we can implement this testing  uh certainly we could uh you know  basketball  um it looks like the ncaa is going to uh  change the start date of basketball  season from november 10th to probably  november 25th or later so that's  probably the first decision  we got to take can we revisit the  decision not play basketball before  january 1st  and maybe start the season at the same  date or close to  when the rest of the country is and then  on a parallel  path we've got a way up does it make  sense to start the football season in  late november  early december or does it make sense to  you know get through finals  for the student athletes get through the  christmas holidays and start in january  one of our considerations gonna be um  you know trying to move in tandem with  the big ten  if we can it's a little unclear where  they are what they're gonna do despite  me talking to their commissioner  every day but um you know if the two of  our conferences can move together we've  got  pretty exciting vision of playing you  know big ten pac-12 seasons  finishing with a pac-12 championship big  ten championship  and then playing a rose bowl and other  bowl games at the end of the season  even if it's an early spring game so  ohio state  or michigan or whoever in the big ten  wisconsin leading this charge of maybe  coming back this fall  but you're talking about trying to be in  conjunction with the big ten  in the spring or winter spring not fall  correct yeah  well we've laid out that you know vision  that it would be there would be benefits  in us moving in tandem  obviously we don't control that as it's  been clear each one of the conferences  makes their own decisions  so look we've got to stay nimble uh and  flexible and there's a dynamic situation  i think it'll it'll evolve over the next  couple weeks i think there'll be more  clarity  on the big ten side i know they're  hopeful to get access  to the kind of daily rapid result  testing that we have which again  yeah real breakthrough in terms of  medical  community at least in the big ten and  pac-12 being comfortable  with a resumption of play um and if we  can move together that would be best if  we can't we can't we could we can only  control what we control  but also if you're looking at uh the  president's had a vote in the big ten  and i don't know what role the  commissioner had you know kevin warren  had  uh the same with the pac-12 are you  going to have your presidents  voting on this or do you decide on if  there's going to be  false sports basketball if there's going  to be winter sports uh spring sports  who decides it works the same way in in  all of our leagues  the presidents ultimately decide they're  the board okay  so yeah we're we're the chief executives  we're expected to do the groundwork to  make a recommendation at the end of the  day they decide how much pressure is on  you  it's a tremendous amount of pressure i  mean there's been so much anxiety  and uncertainty um you know we're  responsible for  a lot of people whether it's in our  conference office  at the universities and then 7 000  student athletes and every one of those  groups  has been dealing in a world of uh  anxiety and uncertainty  you know for our student athletes  they're the ones i'm concerned most  about i mean  being a student athlete being able to  participate in your sport is an  important part of like who you are  and your identity and and going to  school and so it's been heart-wrenching  to see these opportunities be postponed  for these student athletes who are eager  to play but at the same time  they expect us as the adults in the room  and their parents there and i've spoken  to their parents too  on zoom calls and others you know expect  us to have their health and well-being  in mind  not just for the season but you know for  the rest of their life too  yeah i thought that uh the pac-12 was  dead uh you guys  are breathing you know some life back  into this sports program you guys were  quiet and i didn't know if that was a  good thing  uh the big ten is quiet right now and i  don't know if that's a good thing but  it feels like that when you guys are  quiet that things are happening behind  the scenes there  and um i think that was the case here  yeah  well we we've certainly been uh working  hard  at this um trying to solve for these  things you know we've been more measured  than other leagues you know we've let  the science and data  guide us uh there's a lot of emotion  around this too but we've tried to block  that out  and you know on the on on the west coast  um you know i think our universities  have athletics i think in a pretty  healthy  perspective um you know you see the part  of the broader  you know community of our universities  and uh we're gonna follow the  the health and medical uh guidelines and  not not make exceptions so  that's why we had to wait until the kind  of thing we announced yesterday which  changes the game for us  uh because we can look any constituency  in the eye and say  we are not putting your son and daughter  at risk  but how do you explain this though  commissioner that the sec  and the big 12 and the acc are playing  through this  same information i'm going to guess it's  same information  and uh look i you know there's a lot of  pressure on everyone  and you know there's different  priorities different parts of the  country so  uh you know i respect whatever decisions  they're making they're under different  pressures  you know um uh but yeah i think college  football is a microcosm of what's going  on in our country  you know the same data in science is out  there and governors and other officials  are making different decisions about  mask wearing  whether bars should be open or not  whether k-12  should be open or not so um you know not  not a great moment in our country  in that regard that the same science and  data is being applied differently and  college football is not an exception  yeah that's what i i've  said to as many people as possible why  can't we get  the pac-12 commissioners and have their  medical teams  get together on a zoom call just to be  able to exchange ideas larry  i this doesn't make sense and it's  i know that there's priorities that are  different in the south than they are in  the west and then the midwest i get all  of that  but man we can't play political games  with this  we want to play games not political  games i agree what i will tell you is  there is an amazing collaboration  between the  conferences including the medical  advisers they speak every week  so the information's being shared you  know it's at the decision-making level  you know you asked before who makes the  decision it's the presidents and  chancellors of universities  and if you're part of a public  university you've got a higher authority  you work for too right your state yeah  so uh just that has to be kept in mind  you know the  the commissioners our medical advisors  are very very collaborative  we've tried to move together but when  the decisions get made  you know they're made by the leaders of  our universities  but if all goes well commissioner when  is the pac-12 playing football  well right now um i've got high degree  confidence we're playing  in january okay it's possible because  this announcement we  we could play sooner so i think the  significance of yesterday's announcement  having access to this testing  is you know i can look in the eye  virtually and tell you i've got a high  degree of confidence we're playing  just on a post bone base we're gonna  have a full exciting season  uh for our student athletes well i  shouldn't say full as in  playing 12 games i think it will be  abbreviated like it is in other  conferences  um what's now also possible is we could  start earlier than january but that's  going to depend on some things outside  our control like public health  authorities and  and making the decision does it make  sense to start at the end of the year  just wait to the beginning of the year  how are you doing fully recovered from  uh coronavirus  thanks for asking yep that was just  another layer of anxiety i went through  it my whole family got it  um uh it spread very quickly through our  household but  thankfully fully recovered several weeks  ago and got my stamina back and back to  working out but  yeah there's a lot of unknowns about  this certainly with all the members of  my family it affected everyone a little  differently  good to visit with you have a good  weekend and uh good luck with this  thanks dan appreciate you having me  that's uh larry scott back 12  commissioner

